---
import PostLayout from '../../layouts/PostLayout.astro';
import PostFigure from '../../components/PostFigure.astro';

import travelWireframes from '../../assets/images/travel-sd-wireframes.png';
---

<PostLayout slug="ai-in-the-design-process">
	<p>
		A lot of design work is slow but not hard. Reading transcripts, tagging notes, turning sticky notes into themes,
		writing up specs, documenting components. It all matters, but it competes for time with the work that needs
		judgement: deciding which problem matters, what to test, and what the evidence really says.
	</p>
	<p>
		That's where I use AI. Not to design for me, but to take on the slow parts so I can spend more time on the
		judgement.
	</p>

	<h2>Faster synthesis, same standard</h2>
	<p>
		Research synthesis is one of the most time-consuming parts of discovery. AI tools can now help tag observations,
		find patterns across participants and draft a summary. A round of research that used to take days to synthesise
		can be turned around in hours.
	</p>
	<p>
		The rule I keep: AI drafts, a person decides. I read the transcripts behind any pattern it suggests, check its
		themes against what I saw in the sessions, and rewrite anything that doesn't hold up. It's a fast first pass, not
		the answer.
	</p>

	<h2>First-draft journey maps</h2>
	<p>
		AI can also turn raw research notes into a first draft of a journey map structure: stages, steps, pain points and
		quotes. That gives the team something to react to and correct in a workshop, instead of a blank board. The map
		still gets checked against the evidence, step by step.
	</p>

	<h2>Testing ideas sooner</h2>
	<p>
		AI-assisted prototyping means an idea can be in front of a customer much earlier. Some tools can recreate a live
		product and add a new feature into it, so people test it in context rather than against an abstract mock-up.
		Design tools can also help with variants, accessibility checks and spec notes, which cuts down documentation time.
	</p>
	<PostFigure
		src={travelWireframes}
		alt="Travel booking wireframes showing search, detail, payment and confirmation screens"
		caption="Early wireframes. AI helps get ideas like these in front of people sooner."
	/>

	<h2>Making knowledge easy to find</h2>
	<p>
		The most useful thing I've built with AI wasn't a design at all. As our team got smaller, a lot of knowledge lived
		in individual heads. I connected the design system documentation, journey maps, research repository, live products
		and internal standards, such as accessibility guidelines, brand and legal requirements, into one source the team
		could ask questions of through chat. A designer in a squad could ask a question where they were already working and
		get an answer drawn from every source, instead of searching four tools.
	</p>

	<h2>Where I don't trust it</h2>
	<p>
		AI is confident even when it's wrong. I don't let it stand in for talking to real customers, invent research
		findings, or make the final call on what the evidence means. I'm careful with customer data and follow the
		organisation's privacy rules about what can go into which tool. And I treat anything it writes as a draft that
		still needs a designer's eye.
	</p>
	<p>
		Used this way, AI doesn't replace the design process. It makes each loop faster, which means better information
		reaches product decisions sooner, and being wrong costs less.
	</p>
</PostLayout>
